# Chapter 21: The High Tide of Imperialism
# Structured Notes in Nodelang Syntax

new imperialism (new_imp) : 19th century Western expansion into Asia and Africa for markets and raw materials

new_imp < (What caused it?) The Industrial Revolution created material demands and export needs
new_imp < (What justified it?) Social Darwinism and "White Man's Burden" ideology
new_imp < (What enabled it?) Quinine and firearms gave technological advantage
new_imp < (What role did religion play?) Missionary conversion efforts

Industrial Revolution (ind_rev) : Demanded raw materials and export markets
ind_rev <directly_caused> new_imp

colonial system (col_sys) : Methods for governing conquered territories

col_sys < (What were the primary methods?) 1. Direct rule: European officials replace local elites\n2. Indirect rule: Rule through local collaborators\n
col_sys < (Cultural transformation approach) Assimilation: Transform colonial societies into Western model
col_sys < (Cultural preservation approach) Association: Collaborate while preserving local traditions

british colonization of india (raj) : British rule in India after Mughal decline

raj < (What were the reforms?) Education, infrastructure, outlawed sati and thuggee
raj < (What was the exploitation?) Zamindar tax system and textile industry destruction
raj < (How did Indians resist?) 1857 Sepoy Rebellion over greased cartridges
raj < (What was the educational impact?) Macaulay's "English in taste" elite

southeast asian colonization (se_asia) : European colonization of Southeast Asia

se_asia < (French territories) Indochina: Vietnam, Cambodia, Laos
se_asia < (British territories) Malaya, Singapore, Burma
se_asia < (Dutch territories) East Indies with indirect rule through aristocracy
se_asia < (American territories) Philippines after Spanish-American War
se_asia < (Independent state) Thailand remained free through diplomacy

scramble for africa (scramble) : Rapid European colonization after 1880s

scramble < (What triggered it?) King Leopold's personal claim to Congo
scramble < (What were the rules?) Berlin Conference 1884-85 set effective occupation principle
scramble < (What was British strategy?) Control of Nile Valley to protect Suez Canal
scramble < (Who successfully resisted?) Ethiopia's independence at Battle of Adowa 1896
scramble < (What were the underlying motives?) European rivalries, missionary factor, technological superiority

king leopold's congo (leopold) : Personal rule with brutal rubber exploitation

leopold < (What justified it?) "Small country needs colony for image"
leopold < (How was it investigated?) Casement Commission 1903-04

british egypt and sudan (egypt) : Control of Nile Valley

egypt < (Strategic asset) Suez Canal completed 1869, British control by 1875
egypt < (Challenged by) 1. Mahdi revolt 1881-85 killed General Gordon\n2. Fashoda incident 1898 confrontation with France

anticolonial resistance (anti_col) : Subject peoples' responses to colonial rule

anti_col < (Traditional form) Led by existing ruling class, defending traditional institutions
anti_col < (Modern form) Nationalism among educated elites
anti_col < (Alternative path) Collaboration with colonial powers

traditional resistance (trad_res) : Defense of traditional institutions against invaders

trad_res < (Indian examples) Local rulers' guerrilla tactics, Sepoy Rebellion
trad_res < (African examples) Mahdi revolt, Zulu wars, Ashanti resistance
trad_res < (Outcome) Usually failed against superior weapons

modern nationalism (nationalism) : Awareness of shared identity among educated elites

nationalism < (Originated among) Lawyers, teachers, journalists, doctors
nationalism < (Indian pioneer) Ram Mohan Roy's Brahmo Samaj 1828

imperialism legacy evaluation (legacy) : Complex assessment of colonialism's impact

legacy < (Critics' verdict) Self-interested, brutal, denied self-determination
legacy < (Defenders' argument) Laid groundwork for modernization
legacy < (Overall assessment) Advanced technology but at tremendous human cost
